.TH SINDOOK-REWRAP 1 "2026-08-31" "sindook 0.11.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sindook-rewrap \- rotate recipients, passphrases, or the file key
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sindook rewrap
[\fB\-i\fP \fIIDENTITY\fP | \fB\-p\fP | \fB\-passfile\fP \fIFILE\fP]
[\fB\-identity-passfile\fP \fIFILE\fP] (\fB\-r\fP \fIRECIPIENT\fP)...
[\fB\-R\fP \fIFILE\fP]... [\fB\-new-passphrase\fP |
\fB\-new-passfile\fP \fIFILE\fP] [\fB\-glob\fP \fIPATTERN\fP]...
[\fB\-deep\fP] [\fB\-o\fP \fIOUT\fP] [\fB\-f\fP] \fIFILE\fP...
.SH DESCRIPTION
Replaces the key slots of sealed files. With no unlocking credential
flag, the identity selected by
.BR sindook-init (1)
is used when one exists. By default fast mode preserves
the payload ciphertext without decrypting or re-encrypting it, then
copies that ciphertext to a replacement file with a fresh header.
Fast mode does not revoke someone who kept a copy of the old file.
\fB\-deep\fP creates a replacement with a fresh file key, so removed
recipients cannot open that replacement using the old file key.
Files are staged beside their original path and replaced only after a
successful write unless \fB\-o\fP is given. Rotating a whole directory
of files in one run is the intended use. Fast rewrap also upgrades v1
files to the current format in place.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-i\fP \fIIDENTITY\fP
identity that opens the files today; use \fB@default\fP for the
identity selected by
.BR sindook-init (1) ;
with no credential flag at all, that default identity is used when
one exists
.TP
\fB\-p\fP
open with the current passphrase (prompted)
.TP
\fB\-passfile\fP \fIFILE\fP
read the current passphrase from \fIFILE\fP
.TP
\fB\-identity-passfile\fP \fIFILE\fP
read a protected identity's passphrase from \fIFILE\fP
.TP
\fB\-r\fP \fIRECIPIENT\fP
new recipient, repeatable
.TP
\fB\-R\fP \fIFILE\fP
file of new recipients, one key per line, repeatable
.TP
\fB\-new-passphrase\fP
add a new passphrase slot (prompted)
.TP
\fB\-new-passfile\fP \fIFILE\fP
read the new passphrase from \fIFILE\fP
.TP
\fB\-glob\fP \fIPATTERN\fP
add files matched by a portable filesystem pattern, repeatable
.TP
\fB\-deep\fP
re-encrypt the payload under a fresh file key
.TP
\fB\-o\fP \fIOUT\fP
output path, \fB\-\fP for stdout (single \fIFILE\fP only)
.TP
\fB\-f\fP
overwrite existing output
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
sindook rewrap -i my.key -r alice.pub -r bob.pub archive.tar.sindook
sindook rewrap -i my.key -r alice.pub -deep archive.tar.sindook
sindook rewrap -i old.key -R team.keys backups/*.sindook
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sindook (1),
.BR sindook-seal (1),
.BR sindook-open (1)
